# Project context table — what to feed the agent and when. Same format as
# hooks/write_advice.rules: one line — one rule, three fields separated by a vertical bar.
#
#   <when> | <target masks, comma-separated> | <project document section heading>
#
#   when = "start" — HOT CORE: the section is fed at session start, resume, after
#           /clear and after compaction. The masks field of such lines is EMPTY: they are bound to the event,
#           not to a write target.
#   when = <kind of work> — COLD LAYER: the section is fed before a write to a file matching the
#           mask. The mask is matched against the file NAME, not the path.
#
# The heading is matched by PREFIX: "Known pitfalls" matches both a short heading and
# "## Known pitfalls / mines (do not rediscover…)". An exact match would require every
# project to rewrite the tail character for character.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ENGINE DEFAULT. A project declares its own in `<project>/.retainer/context.rules`, and
# the project table REPLACES this one entirely — including the "start" lines. They cannot be forgotten there unnoticed:
# the `hot_context` gate step goes red on a project table without the hot core.
#
# A line starting with a hash is a comment. Adding a rule means adding a line.

start |  | What NOT to do
start |  | Known pitfalls

# COLD lines are absent here and cannot be present: the "kind of work → section" mapping is known only to the
# project itself — in one, layout pitfalls live in "Known pitfalls", in another in a separate section.
# The form is this (copy into `<project>/.retainer/context.rules` and edit for your headings):
#
#   layout    | *.html,*.htm,*.css,*.jinja2,*.vue | Layout pitfalls
#   migrations | *.sql,000*.py                    | Known pitfalls
